 { Comparison Herman Ottevaere – Karel Falleyn, Correspondence 1973 Black to play The knight on c6 is hanging and taking the rook with } ( 30. Nxb4 $2 { is refuted immediately by the beautiful } Nd5 $3 { And taking the pawn first with } ) 
 ( 30. Rxh7 $6 { is met with } Rxg4 $1 { which is quite simply a mess. } ) 
 ( 30. c4 $5 { is not a bad move, but also not totally clear. } ) 
 { What White needs to do is to protect the c6-knight with the queen, but is this best done from g2 or h1? The method of comparison is very useful for the practical player. Let’s assume that you can have the same position except with the pawn on either a2 or b2; and that there are a lot of variations to calculate. In such a scenario it is a great help to be able to think in concepts, trying to make out what the differences will be between the two different variations. We will be able to calculate a lot of tactics and then be able to put in our take on them. }  
 *

