 { Herman Ottevaere – Karel Falleyn, Correspondence 1973 } { _ } 30. Qg2 $6 { This game was played in the glory days of correspondence chess when it was possible to completely miss your opponent’s next move. I have a feeling this happened a few times to White, but it is certainly possible to solve this position using both conceptual thinking and straight calculation. As we shall see below, the correct move was 30 Qh1!! after which White is completely winning. } ( 30. Qh1 $3 d5 31. Nxb4 Qc3 32. Bd4 Qxd4 33. Nc4 $1 Qc3 34. Rd3 Qxb4 35. g5 { with extra material and a winning attack both on the kingside and in the centre. White could also take on h7 on the previous move or on d5 here. The choices are many. A possible end could arise after } Ne4 36. Rxe4 $1 dxe4 37. Qxe4 Ra6 ( 37. Qg2 Re8 38. Qe6 Kh8 39. fxg6 { with a mating attack; Rd3-h3 is on its way. } ) 
38. f6 $1 exf6 39. Rd8 Bf8 40. Qd5 Kg7 41. Rd7 Kh8 42. Qf7 { with mate. } ) 
d5 $1 { This was obviously Black’s idea. } ( 30... Rc8 $6 31. Nxb4 Qc3 { is weaker. The rook is better on a8 and the pawn is useful on d5. } 32. Rb1 ( 31... d5 31. Nc4 Rxc4 33. Nd3 { also wins. } ) 
Qxb4 33. c4 { and White wins. } ) 
31. Nxb4 ( 31. c4 $2 Qxc6 32. g5 { does not work. Black is much better after } Ne4 $1 { With the idea } 33. Nxe4 Rxb3 $3 { with a mating attack. } ) 
Qc3 32. Nc4 $4 { It would be easy to dismiss this move as a tactical mistake, overlooking Black’s combination. This would not take into account the great transformation of chess thinking that has happened in the last twenty years with the rise and rise of the chess engines. Today people would be looking for a so-called 'computer move' to try to save the game, and in this case, they would find it: } ( 32. Bd4 $3 Qxd4 { Now } 33. Nc4 $2 { would hang the rook on d1 and lose quite quickly. But if White had played 30 Qh1!! instead of 30 Qg2?!, play would most likely have been more or less identical with the queen’s placement being the only difference. So the rook on d1 would be defended and the game would end quickly after 33 Nc4! () But White did not play this, so we have to put the queen back on the inferior g2-square. White’s options are limited. In this position, he should play: } ( 33. c3 $1 { This would allow White to stay in the game; and even play for an advantage. } Qxc3 34. Nc4 Rb8 ( 34. Nc4 Qxb4 35. Rxd5 { also favours White, but is not entirely clear. } ) 
35. Rh3 $1 Qxb4 36. Rxd5 e6 $1 { The only move. } ( 36. Nc4 gxf5 37. gxf5 h5 38. Rdd3 { and Rdg3 wins. } ) 
37. Rdd3 exf5 38. gxf5 $16 { White has a strong advantage and should probably win, but a lot of tricks still exist. } ) 
 ) 
Ne4 $1 { Black is winning, as no doubt both players believed all along. } 33. Nxd5 { The mate arising after } ( 33. Bd4 Bxd4 34. Rxd4 dxc4 { is a bit more prosaic. But } ( 33. fxg6 { would have allowed a very pretty finish in: } Qa1 $1 34. Rxa1 Nc3 ) 
 { White has an extra knight, rook and queen, but little does it help him! } ) 
Qb2+ $1 { White resigned. To sum up – the queen is better placed on h1, from where it is both attacking h7 and, far more importantly, defending the rook on d1. Some people would be able to solve this by pure calculation, while others would find it easier to solve this by thinking in general concepts. Obviously, there are drawbacks to both forms of thinking, but combined they can be quite powerful. } 
 0-1

