 { The final example before we move on to the exercises is quite complex and in many ways a logical continuation of the previous example. Why will become obvious along the way. The first thing to say is that Black is in a difficult situation. However, by using the method of comparison, he manages to save the game. In Position A, Black has been able to bring his queen to the great central square e5, from where it both dominates the queenside and protects the kingside. }  { While in Position B, Black is one move away from putting his queen on e5, while White’s queen and rook are placed differently. From a practical point of view I would steer away from Position A without calculating further. The rook is passive on e1 and the various tactical ideas look less dangerous with the queen on e3. It looks far more aggressive on f3, where it is shooting at h5 and f7. But once I come back home after a game, I like to back up such logic with concrete analysis in order to improve my intuition. I am quite sure that Smerdon had done this thoroughly in his preparation in advance of this game and this is where he found a stellar resource. But let’s start by looking at the most obvious variation... } 
 *

