 { For Position B it is much harder to calculate the lines, as they are less forcing, at least on an immediate basis. But once you get deep into the position, you will see that the queen is not worse placed on e3. Leinier Dominguez Perez – David Smerdon, Khanty Mansiysk (1.4) 2009 It is obvious that Black has to take the knight on b5 as his knight on f3 cannot be saved. But the circumstances are open to manipulation. } 26... Ne1 $3 { A nice desperado move. I have often noticed that a very good way to calculate desperado situations (where everything is hanging) is to look for ways to sidestep the constant slugging of the pieces. With this move Smerdon steers towards Position B, while } ( 26... Qxb5 27. Qxf3 Qe5 { would have landed him in Position A. } ) 
27. Rxe1 Qxb5 { We have arrived at Position B. Dominguez is known as a ferocious attacking player, but he did not find a way to cause sufficient damage to the black king’s position. } 28. c3 $6 { Not terribly energetic. If we look at the variations we will see that White is actually close to winning here as well, but Black’s use of the comparison strategy made it difficult for his opponent to win the game. } ( 28. Rh1 $1 { The intention is obvious. White wants to take on h5. Here the natural move would be to centralize the queen and bring it closer to the defence. } Qe5 $2 { If we compare this with Position A then the position of the white queen is the only difference. This is quite important when we evaluate the exchange sacrifice: } 29. Rxh5 ( 29. Qf3 { could probably be met with something hilarious like } b5 { when the pawn is better placed than in Position A far down the line. But more importantly, Black has } ( 29. c3 Qf4 30. Qd3 Qe5 { and no particular problems. The computer values White’s position very highly, but I cannot see that this is justified. } ) 
 ) 
gxh5 30. g6 { With the queen at e3 instead of f3, we spot the extra option of Qh6!, but also that White is unable to deliver mate on f7. Black, therefore, does not have 30...f6 this time around, but he can try something else. } Kf8 $5 { Trying to run away; unashamedly. } 31. gxf7 $1 { The strongest move. Black will slowly be ground down with his open king and the many threats on the light squares. } ( 31. Rf2 $2 Kg7 32. Rxf7 Kxg6 33. Re7 Re8 34. Rxb7 Rg8 { is roughly even, but certainly unclear. } ) 
 ( 31. Qf3 $2 f6 $1 { ⩱ is also nothing for White. } ) 
d5 ( 31. c3 Ke7 32. Rg5 $1 Qf6 33. Qg1 Qf4 34. Rg6 $1 { is awful for Black. } ) 
32. a3 Ke7 ( 32. c3 Qxe4 33. Qg5 Qd4 34. c3 Qh8 35. Bc2 { and Black cannot hold this position forever. } ) 
33. Qb6 Qxe4 34. Qxb7 Kd6 35. Qa6 Ke7 36. Re2 Qf5 37. Ka2 { White is winning in the long run. Black’s king is too exposed and there is no way for him to activate his rooks and make his extra exchange felt. On } ) 
 ( 28. Rh1 { Black could try } Rc7 $1 { in order to anticipate the sacrifice on h5 and defend the 7th rank. This was pointed out to me by David Smerdon and was not included in the previous editions of this book, which looked a bit different to this edition. A possible variation could be: } 29. a4 Qe5 30. Rxh5 gxh5 31. g6 f6 32. Qh6 Rg7 33. Rh2 Qxe4 34. Qxh5 Qxg6 35. Bxe6 Kf8 36. Qa5 Re8 37. Rh8 Ke7 38. Bc4 Qg1 39. Ka2 Qc5 40. Rxe8 Kxe8 41. Qa8 Ke7 42. Qxb7 Kf8 { The game continues, with a draw being the most reasonable result. } ) 
Qe5 29. a3 ( 29. Rf1 b5 30. Qf3 Rc7 { looks okay for Black. } ) 
b5 30. Ka2 Kg7 31. Rf1 Rc7 32. Qf3 Ra8 33. Rh1 Rh8 34. Rd1 Rd8 35. Rh1 Rh8 36. Rd1 Rd8 37. Rh1 { Great preparation by Smerdon! } 
